# Line endings must be identical on every platform, and the bytes stored in
# the object database must be the bytes we published.
#
# 1. autoconf treats CR as data. With core.autocrlf=true (the GitHub Actions
#    default on windows-latest) configure.ac landed with CRLF and aclocal died
#    with: configure.ac:798: error: '\<CR>' is already registered with
#    AC_CONFIG_FILES. eol=lf pins the worktree to LF regardless of the
#    user's autocrlf setting.
#
# 2. Everything imported from the third-party forks is verified by SHA-256
#    against manifest/PROVENANCE.json. Those digests describe the files as
#    the forks published them, and every one of those files is LF. With
#    "text=auto" git normalises the worktree copy to LF on commit and checks
#    out LF everywhere, so the stored blob is the published file again and
#    the integrity gate can tell a real edit from a checkout artefact.
#
# 3. The three binary objects must never be touched. "text=auto" already
#    detects them by the NUL byte, but they are listed explicitly as well so
#    the intent is obvious and so a future heuristic change cannot break it.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png   -text
*.bit   -text
*.bin   -text
